  "worked_solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x-(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x})y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x)}_{\u23df}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}_{\u23df}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
